<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">State of the Presidi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1557">1557</a>–<a href="/years/1801">1801</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Stato dei Presidi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The State of the Presidi was a group of fortified coastal places in southern Tuscany, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orbetello">Orbetello</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-ercole">Porto Ercole</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porto-santo-stefano">Porto Santo Stefano</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talamone">Talamone</a>, kept by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> when it granted Siena to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo I de' Medici</a> in 1557. Garrisoned to guard the sea route between Spain's Italian possessions, it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and then to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, and was annexed to the French-backed Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/etruria">Etruria</a> in 1801.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/state-of-the-presidi.jpg" alt="State of the Presidi" width="320" height="494" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Wilkinson, London, 1800. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tuscany,_Piombino,_Elba,_State_of_the_Presidios,_1800.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
